- North American beavers (Castor canadensis) build dams on streams and rivers.
- A single beaver family can fell up to 300 trees per year.
- Beaver dams create ponds that can cover 2 to 25 hectares.
- These ponds support wetland ecosystems used by amphibians, fish, waterfowl, and aquatic invertebrates.
- As of 2023, an estimated 10 to 15 million beavers live in North America.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"North American beavers are known for building dams, which create ponds in streams and rivers across the continent."},{"key":"B","text":"By felling up to 300 trees per year and creating ponds spanning 2 to 25 hectares, a single beaver family reshapes the landscape on a scale few other animals can match."},{"key":"C","text":"The ponds created by beaver dams provide habitat for amphibians, fish, waterfowl, and aquatic invertebrates that depend on wetland ecosystems."},{"key":"D","text":"Researchers estimate that between 10 and 15 million beavers currently inhabit North America, making them one of the continent's most widespread rodents."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to emphasize the scale of beavers'' environmental impact. The choice that cites specific quantities — 300 trees per year and 2 to 25 hectares of pond coverage — directly conveys scale through concrete numbers. The option about dam-building generally describes what beavers do without emphasizing magnitude. The option about habitat lists the species that benefit, addressing ecological diversity rather than scale. The option about population numbers describes how many beavers exist, not the scale of their environmental effect.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Ornithologist David Enríquez has documented that European starlings in urban environments produce song repertoires approximately 40 percent more complex than their rural counterparts. Enríquez attributes this difference to the acoustic masking effect of city noise, which pressures urban birds to develop more varied vocal strategies to communicate effectively.

Text 2
A 2022 study by acoustic ecologist Sumi Patel recorded starling populations in three mid-sized cities before and after municipal noise ordinances reduced ambient traffic sound by an average of 9 decibels. Patel found no significant change in song complexity following the noise reduction, suggesting that once established in a population, elevated vocal complexity persists independently of the environmental pressure that initially drove it.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By arguing that urban noise has no measurable effect on starling vocalizations."},{"key":"B","text":"By suggesting that once vocal complexity develops in response to noise, it is maintained even after noise levels decrease."},{"key":"C","text":"By claiming that rural starlings produce more complex songs than urban starlings."},{"key":"D","text":"By proposing that all starling populations produce equally varied song repertoires regardless of habitat."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'Patel''s study found that song complexity persisted after noise was reduced, which does not deny that noise originally caused the complexity (as Enríquez argues) but adds the nuance that the effect is maintained even when the initial pressure is removed. Arguing that urban noise has no measurable effect is wrong because Patel''s findings do not dispute the original relationship between noise and complexity; they show the complexity persists after noise reduction, which is a different claim. Claiming rural starlings are more complex is wrong because both texts agree that urban starlings have more complex repertoires. Proposing equal repertoires across habitats is wrong because Patel''s own data show urban populations maintained elevated complexity, confirming a habitat-linked difference.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

Psycholinguist Ana Varga has proposed that bilingual speakers do not maintain two separate lexical systems but instead operate from a single integrated lexicon in which words from both languages compete for activation during speech production. According to Varga, the stronger the activation level of a word in one language, the more it inhibits the retrieval of its translation equivalent, explaining why bilinguals occasionally experience cross-language interference even in monolingual conversational settings.

Text 2
In a 2023 eye-tracking study, cognitive scientist Dmitri Orlov presented balanced bilinguals with sentences containing a temporarily ambiguous word—one whose initial phonemes matched a high-frequency word in the participants'' other language. Orlov found that participants fixated on competitor objects associated with the cross-language match for an average of 173 milliseconds longer than on unrelated objects, but only when the surrounding sentence context was semantically unconstrained. When sentence context strongly predicted the target word, the cross-language interference effect disappeared entirely.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It refutes the proposal by demonstrating that bilingual speakers maintain entirely separate lexical systems."},{"key":"B","text":"It extends the proposal by showing that cross-language interference increases proportionally with sentence complexity."},{"key":"C","text":"It provides evidence that partially supports the proposal while identifying a condition under which the described interference does not operate."},{"key":"D","text":"It replicates the findings described in Text 1 using a different population of bilingual speakers."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Orlov''s study both supports and qualifies Varga''s proposal: the eye-tracking data confirm that cross-language interference occurs (supporting the integrated-lexicon model), but the interference disappears when sentence context is strongly constraining (identifying a boundary condition Varga''s proposal does not address). Refuting the proposal entirely is wrong because Orlov''s data do show cross-language interference in unconstrained contexts, which is consistent with a shared lexicon rather than separate systems. Increasing interference with sentence complexity is wrong because the study found the opposite—constraining context eliminated interference rather than amplifying it. Replicating the findings of Text 1 is wrong because Text 1 describes a theoretical proposal rather than experimental findings, and Orlov''s results add a qualifying condition rather than simply confirming the proposal.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

- Marine biologists have traditionally assumed that coral reefs in cooler, deeper waters (mesophotic reefs, 30–150 meters deep) could serve as refugia for shallow-water coral species threatened by warming.
- A 2022 study by Dr. Pim Bongaerts of the California Academy of Sciences examined genetic data from 17 coral species across depth gradients in the Caribbean.
- The study found that mesophotic corals are largely genetically distinct populations, not depth-displaced versions of shallow-water corals.
- Only 3 of the 17 species showed significant genetic connectivity between shallow and mesophotic populations.
- The finding suggests that mesophotic reefs cannot replenish shallow reefs after bleaching events as previously hoped.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Dr. Pim Bongaerts of the California Academy of Sciences analyzed genetic data from 17 coral species across depth gradients in the Caribbean, finding that only 3 species showed significant genetic connectivity between shallow and mesophotic populations."},{"key":"B","text":"Marine biologists have traditionally viewed mesophotic reefs, which exist at depths of 30 to 150 meters, as potential refugia for shallow-water coral species, but recent genetic evidence indicates these deep reefs may be unable to fulfill that role."},{"key":"C","text":"Although mesophotic reefs were long expected to serve as refugia that could replenish bleached shallow reefs, a 2022 genetic study of 17 Caribbean coral species revealed that deep and shallow populations are largely distinct — with only 3 species showing significant cross-depth connectivity — undermining that assumption."},{"key":"D","text":"Coral reefs at depths of 30 to 150 meters, known as mesophotic reefs, harbor genetically distinct populations of coral that differ from their shallow-water counterparts, a finding with important implications for conservation strategies."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The goal is to present a finding that challenges a commonly held assumption. The choice beginning with ''Although mesophotic reefs were long expected'' explicitly states the prior assumption, presents the specific genetic evidence (17 species, only 3 with connectivity), and directly frames the result as undermining that assumption — fulfilling all elements of the rhetorical goal. The option about Dr. Bongaerts''s study reports the data but omits the assumption being challenged, so it does not frame the finding as contradicting a prior belief. The option mentioning traditional views and recent evidence identifies the assumption and signals a challenge but lacks the specific data (the 3-of-17 ratio) that makes the challenge concrete. The option describing genetically distinct populations presents the finding neutrally without referencing or challenging the refugia assumption.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

